{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 33, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "the social network, directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, stands as a defining film of the digital age. it chronicles the tumultuous rise of facebook and the legal battles that followed its creation. the movie opens with a rapid-fire dialogue between mark zuckerberg and his girlfriend, instantly establishing the protagonist's sharp intellect and social alienation. jesse eisenberg delivers a captivating performance, portraying zuckerberg not as a villain, but as a complex figure driven by a desire for recognition and belonging.\n\nthe narrative is structured around two simultaneous lawsuits, which allows the story to jump back and forth in time, keeping the pacing relentless and engaging. andrew garfield shines as eduardo saverin, the co-founder whose betrayal serves as the emotional core of the film. the chemistry between the cast members is electric, fueled by sorkin's signature witty and fast-paced script. every conversation feels like a verbal duel, highlighting the high stakes of the tech world in the mid-2000s.\n\nvisually, the film is sleek and cold, mirroring the sterile environment of harvard university and the digital spaces the characters inhabit. trent reznor and atticus ross composed a haunting electronic score that perfectly underscores the tension and isolation felt by the characters. the sound design is meticulous, with the clicking of keyboards and the hum of servers becoming rhythmic elements of the soundtrack.\n\nultimately, the social network is more than just a biography; it is a tragedy about friendship, ambition, and the cost of success. it questions whether one can truly connect with others while building a platform designed to connect the world. the final scene, where zuckerberg refreshes his ex-girlfriend's profile, leaves the audience with a lingering sense of melancholy. it is a masterpiece of modern cinema that